---
import RecipeLayout from '../../layouts/RecipeLayout.astro';
---
<RecipeLayout title="Soft Sandwich Loaf Base">
  <div class="recipe-title-block">
    <h1>Soft Sandwich Loaf Base</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">This one grew up and got a name too.</p>
  </div>

  <main>
    <section class="block">
      <div class="callout">
        <p>The Soft Sandwich Loaf Base is the tangzhong-enriched foundation behind two loaves on this site: a plain, dairy-free version in The Classics, and a milk-and-butter-enriched version in Enriched &amp; Sweet. Both start from the same technique — a small portion of flour and liquid cooked into a thick paste before it ever meets the rest of the dough, giving the finished loaf a soft, pillowy, long-lasting crumb that regular mixing can't replicate on its own.</p>
        <p>The milk-and-butter version has since been given the full recipe treatment and published as <a href="/recipes/breadwinner/">Breadwinner</a>: recipe card, step-by-step tutorial, ingredient history, and a baking playlist — plus a fully documented swap back to the plain water-and-oil version in its Alternatives section, for whichever section of the site you're baking for. That's the place to go for the complete write-up and the dough this base describes.</p>
        <p>In the meantime, the site's <a href="/guides/">baking Guides</a> have plenty on the technique behind it — the <a href="/guides/starter/starter-vs-levain/">Starter vs. Levain</a> and <a href="/guides/starter/starter-types-and-flours/">Starter Types and Flours</a> guides cover the starter side of things, and there's more on shaping a pan loaf in <a href="/guides/proofing-and-shaping/">Proofing and Shaping</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</RecipeLayout>
